        "phase": "1 — Predict Phase (Prior Knowledge Elicitation & Phenomenon Launch)",
        "learnerExperience": "Students enter the room to find two large printed photographs placed on their desks — one of a sufuria of leftover ugali and one of a half-eaten chapati, both showing colourful fuzzy patches and slimy areas. Working individually for 2 minutes (silent think time), each student writes three quick-fire responses in their exercise books: (1) What do you think the patches are? (2) Where did they come from — no one touched the food? (3) Are the patches alive? Students then turn to their seat partner and compare their three answers for 2 minutes, noting where they agree and where they differ. Pairs prepare one shared 'best prediction' statement to report to the class.",
        "teacherMoves": "As students write silently, circulate and READ (do not comment on) responses — note 3–4 interesting or contrasting predictions to cold-call later. After 2 minutes say: 'Now turn to your partner and share your three ideas. You have 2 minutes — go.' [Allow 2 minutes.] Bring the class back: 'I'm going to cold-call five pairs — not to judge your answer but to collect our class's range of ideas.' Cold-call 5 pairs using a lolly-stick draw. After each response, record the key phrase on the whiteboard under the heading PREDICTIONS without comment or correction. Use probing stems: 'Can you say more about where you think it came from?' and 'What makes you think it might — or might not — be alive?' Allow 10 seconds of WAIT TIME after each probe before accepting a response. Do not confirm or deny any prediction — say: 'That is a fascinating idea — we will find out together.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "SILENT THINK → PAIR → COLD-CALL SHARE (Think-Pair-Share variant). This strategy surfaces the full range of prior conceptions — including misconceptions such as 'the food rotted on its own' or 'it came from the air being dirty' — before any instruction occurs. Recording all predictions without judgment creates psychological safety and gives the teacher a formative baseline of where students' mental models currently sit.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicator: Every student has written at least three sentences in their exercise book before pair-share begins. Teacher scans books during circulation — look specifically for (a) students who reference 'germs' or 'bacteria' by name (prior knowledge present), (b) students who attribute the patches to chemical reactions or dirt (misconception to address in Lesson 3), and (c) students who explicitly wonder whether different patch colours mean different organisms (readiness for classification in Lesson 2). Mentally flag at least one student from each category for targeted questions in later phases."
