        "teacherMoves": "Project the two photographs on the board (or display printed A3 copies). Say: 'We are going to be scientists right now. Scientists do not jump to explanations — they first describe carefully what they observe. Look at this photograph. Tell me only what you can SEE — not what you think it is.' Point to the green patch on the ugali: 'What do you notice about the colour here? Is it uniform or patchy?' [WAIT 10 seconds.] Cold-call 3 students. Then point to the black patch on the chapati edge: 'Is this patch the same texture as the green one — describe it.' [WAIT 10 seconds.] Cold-call 2 students. Then ask: 'Some of you wrote that these patches came from the air. What specific evidence in this photograph supports — or challenges — that idea?' [WAIT 15 seconds.] Cold-call 2 students. Write key observed details on the board in a two-column table: OBSERVATION | POSSIBLE EXPLANATION. Explicitly say: 'We are filling the left column only right now — we will fill the right column as we learn more over the next lessons.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "NOTICE / INFER / WONDER (NIW) Protocol. NIW trains students to separate empirical observation from interpretation — a foundational scientific practice (NGSS SEP 1: Asking Questions; SEP 3: Planning and Carrying Out Investigations). By forcing students to articulate what they literally see before explaining it, the teacher interrupts the common habit of jumping to conclusions and models the discipline of evidence-based reasoning that will underpin every investigation in this sub-strand.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicator: During the Phenomena Gallery, students describe physical properties (colour, texture, location, pattern) rather than causes. If students cannot separate observation from inference (e.g., they say 'I see bacteria' rather than 'I see a fuzzy green patch'), the teacher gently redirects: 'That is an inference — what do you actually see with your eyes?' This distinction is tracked as a readiness indicator for the microscopy activities in Lesson 2."
