        "teacherMoves": "After populating the KWN chart from student contributions, explicitly narrate the scientific relevance: 'Everything in the KNOW column represents your starting mental model — it might be right, it might need refining. Everything in the WONDER column is the engine that will drive our next six lessons. In science, good questions are more valuable than quick answers.' Then reveal the driving question on a large poster or projected slide and read it aloud. Say: 'I want three volunteers to tell me — which ONE word in this driving question do you find most confusing or most interesting?' [WAIT 15 seconds.] Cold-call 3 students. Likely target words: 'invisible', 'organisms', 'protect'. For each word cold-called, ask the class: 'Does anyone want to add to that? Turn to your partner — 30 seconds.' Do NOT explain the words yet — record them on the board as 'words we will define together.' Finally, ask: 'On a scale of 1–3 with your fingers — 1 means I have no idea what is going on, 3 means I feel like I could start explaining this — show me where you are right now.' Scan the room. This is a quick confidence temperature-check, not a grade.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "KWN CHART (Know / Wonder / Notice). The KWN chart is a metacognitive sensemaking tool that externalises the class's collective prior knowledge and makes it visible, revisable and comparable over time. Unlike a simple brainstorm, the separation of 'what we think we know' from 'what we notice' trains students to distinguish prior belief from direct evidence — a critical distinction in scientific reasoning. The chart remains posted throughout the sub-strand and is updated at the end of each lesson.",
