        "teacherMoves": "Distribute sticky notes and say: 'Write one genuine question per note — something you actually want to know. Your question must connect to these photographs. You have 2 minutes — go.' [Allow 2 minutes of silent writing.] Direct students to the DQB: 'Walk up, stick your notes anywhere on the board, then spend 2 minutes reading others' questions and ticking any you also want answered.' After the gallery walk, say: 'I am going to group these questions with you. Call out if you see two questions that are really asking the same thing.' Facilitate clustering with student input — do not impose categories, let them emerge. Once four clusters are visible, say: 'These four clusters are our roadmap for the sub-strand. In Lesson 2 we start answering Cluster 1 — What are the patches? — by looking at them far more closely.' Write 'Lesson 2 ➜' next to Cluster 1 in a different colour to signal the journey ahead. Photograph the DQB with a phone or camera so the record is preserved if notes fall off.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "DRIVING QUESTION BOARD (DQB) — Student-Generated Question Clustering. The DQB is a public, persistent artefact that transforms student curiosity into a structured research agenda. By having students generate and then vote on questions, ownership of the inquiry shifts to the learners. The clustering step models the scientific practice of organising questions by theme (NGSS SEP 1), and the explicit connection of each cluster to a future lesson creates anticipation and a sense of coherent narrative across the sub-strand.",
