        "teacherMoves": "Announce the model-building task: 'I want you to draw your current best explanation — not what you wish you knew, but what you actually think right now. You have 5 minutes. Work in silence.' [Allow 5 minutes.] Circulate and observe — do not correct any model. In the final 1 minute, say: 'Turn to your partner and show them your model — not to judge it, just to see how differently two people can think about the same thing. You have 30 seconds.' Bring the class to attention: 'These models stay in your book. In our last lesson on microorganisms, I will ask you to draw Model 2 — and you will be amazed at how your thinking has grown. The fuzzy patches on that ugali and chapati are going to teach us something remarkable about the invisible world around us every day in Kenya.' Close the lesson by pointing back to the KWN chart and DQB: 'These two tools belong to you. Add to them any time you learn something new — at home, from a parent, from the news. This is a living record of your growing scientific mind.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "INITIAL ANCHOR MODEL (Pre-Instruction Mental Model). Drawing a pre-instruction model activates and makes visible students' existing mental models — including misconceptions — in a low-stakes format. Research in science education shows that students who draw and then revise models across a unit develop significantly deeper conceptual understanding than those who receive direct instruction alone. The 'Model 1 → Model 2' framing creates a built-in self-assessment opportunity and gives the teacher a rich artefact for tracking individual conceptual change across the sub-strand.",
